---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CableDiagram from '../../components/CableDiagram.astro';
import { siteSelection } from '../../release-selection';
import { WAVESHARE } from '../../../installer/release';
const { release, hardwareReleases } = await siteSelection();
const waveshareRelease = hardwareReleases?.[WAVESHARE];
---

<Layout
  title="Getting started"
  description="Find the parts you need, connect your speaker, and set up OpenAthan’s prayer times and volume."
>
  <article class="docs wrap">
    <div class="docs-heading">
      <h1>Getting started</h1><p class="lede">
        The parts you’ll need and the steps to set up your Athan speaker.
      </p>
    </div><div class="docs-body">
      <aside class="notice">
        <strong
          >{
            release
              ? `OpenAthan ${release.tag} is available.`
              : 'New installation is temporarily unavailable.'
          }</strong
        ><p>
          {
            release && (
              <>
                Use <a href="/install/">USB setup</a> to install OpenAthan on the supported
                hardware.{' '}
              </>
            )
          }
          Already have OpenAthan installed? Connect through USB setup to open its settings or recover
          Wi-Fi and password access while keeping your prayer settings and playback history.
        </p>
      </aside><h2>What you’ll need</h2><p>
        Purchase an <a href="https://docs.m5stack.com/en/core/AtomS3R">M5Stack AtomS3R C126</a>{' '}
        and a <a href="https://docs.m5stack.com/en/atom/Voice_Pyramid">Voice Pyramid A167</a> speaker
        base separately. A167 has also been called “Echo Pyramid”; check the SKU when buying. These instructions
        describe this combination of parts. See the <a href="#waveshare-setup"
          >Waveshare Box V2 instructions</a
        > below for that model.
      </p><p>
        You will need a computer with desktop Chrome or Edge, a USB-C <strong>data</strong> cable, and
        a 2.4 GHz WPA2/WPA3 personal Wi-Fi network. A charging-only cable will not work for USB setup.
        Enterprise networks and networks with a sign-in page are not supported.
      </p><CableDiagram /><h2>1. Connect through the Atom</h2><p>
        Attach the modules using their intended connector. Disconnect bottom power, then connect the
        computer to the <strong>Atom’s USB-C port</strong>. Use only this one cable during USB
        setup.
      </p><p>
        Close other serial tools and browser tabs using the device. On <a href="/install/"
          >the installer page</a
        >, select <strong>Choose USB device</strong>, then select the USB JTAG/serial device when
        prompted.
      </p><h2>2. Check the connected speaker</h2><p>
        The website checks for OpenAthan before showing your next steps. An existing OpenAthan opens
        its settings and recovery options. Choose <strong>Open device settings</strong>,
        <strong>Change Wi-Fi</strong>, or create/reset its device password as needed. Recovery
        preserves your prayer settings and playback history.
      </p><p>
        If OpenAthan is not recognized and a release is available, the website shows installation
        confirmation. Detection alone does not prove the device is new or empty. If you expected an
        existing OpenAthan, retry the connection or follow <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/"
          >troubleshooting</a
        >. Continue with a new installation only on supported hardware you intend to erase: confirm
        the hardware and full erasure, then select <strong>Install OpenAthan</strong>. Installation
        replaces existing firmware and saved data; it is not a preserving update.
      </p><p>
        If OpenAthan is recognized but its status cannot be read, reconnect and troubleshoot.
        Installation is blocked to protect the existing device.
      </p><h2>3. Save Wi-Fi and a device password</h2><p>
        After a new installation, select your home network or enter its name for a hidden network,
        then enter its Wi-Fi password. Next, create a separate, long device password. Your browser
        sends these credentials directly over USB; the website does not store them.
      </p><p>
        Remember the device password. You will use the username <strong>admin</strong> to sign into the
        device’s settings.
      </p><h2>4. Move the power cable</h2><p>
        When USB setup finishes, unplug the Atom’s USB cable. Connect <strong
          >only the Pyramid’s bottom USB-C power</strong
        > for normal speaker operation. Wait for Wi-Fi to reconnect.
      </p><h2>5. Finish on the device</h2><p>
        Keep your computer or phone on the same home network and open the device address shown by
        the installer, such as <code>http://openathan-a1b2c3.local/</code>. Each device has its own
        address. If the hostname does not work, try its displayed IP address.
      </p><p>
        Sign in, enter your location and timezone, choose a calculation method, and review the
        prayer times. On current firmware, choose <strong>Find my location</strong> in the device’s settings
        to open the optional <a href="/location/">location helper</a>. Review its suggestion, choose <strong
          >Review on device</strong
        >, then check the coordinates and timezone and choose
        <strong>Preview schedule</strong>. A returned suggestion needs a ready timetable before
        saving; if the clock has not synchronized, wait and preview again. Manual entry is always
        available. On earlier firmware, copy the helper’s values into the settings yourself. Select <strong
          >Finish setup</strong
        > to enable the Athan. If the clock has not synchronized yet, Athan playback will wait.
      </p><h2>Everyday use</h2><p>
        Your speaker calculates prayer times and plays recordings stored on the device. The current
        Atom build needs internet access to set its clock after a restart, before it can play the
        Athan. To open its settings, connect your phone or computer to the same home Wi-Fi. Keep the
        settings page on your trusted home network; do not make it accessible from the internet.
      </p>
      <h2 id="firmware-updates">Update your firmware</h2>
      <p>
        Open your speaker’s settings on the same home Wi-Fi and sign in. In <strong>Firmware</strong
        >, choose <strong>Check for updates</strong>, read the release notes, then choose <strong
          >Install update</strong
        >. The update waits until playback finishes and the next announcement is at least 15 minutes
        away. Keep the speaker powered while it updates and reconnects.
      </p>
      <p>
        Compatible device-page updates preserve your Wi-Fi, device password, prayer settings,
        playback history, light preferences and stored recordings. New recordings in a fresh-install
        release do not replace the recordings on an existing speaker.
      </p>
      <p>
        On v0.2.0, update checks may fail with “Could not check for updates”. Getting the fix
        requires a preserving USB application update. If Firmware is missing or the device reports
        an incompatible bootloader, a preserving USB transition is also required. Follow the
        <a
          href="https://github.com/OpenAthan-Project/openathan/blob/v0.2.1/docs/development/firmware-upgrades.md#startup-recovery-and-existing-devices"
          >preserving upgrade guide</a
        >
        or <a href="https://github.com/OpenAthan-Project/openathan/issues">ask for firmware help</a
        >. Do not use a new installation to update an existing speaker: it erases saved data.
      </p>
      {
        release && (
          <p>
            <a href={`https://github.com/OpenAthan-Project/openathan/releases/tag/${release.tag}`}>
              Read the current release notes
            </a>
            .
          </p>
        )
      }
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/install/">Open device setup</a><a href="/docs/troubleshooting/"
          >Troubleshooting</a
        >
      </div>
      <h2 id="other-hardware">Other hardware</h2>
      <p>
        OpenAthan is designed to keep prayer calculations, scheduling, and settings independent of
        the hardware. Hardware adapters connect these functions to a device’s audio and controls,
        allowing contributors to develop lower-cost builds or add capabilities on other hardware.
      </p>
      <p>
        Audio playback is required. Displays, status lights, buttons, touch controls, microphones, a
        hardware clock, and removable storage are optional capabilities. Standalone operation does
        not require Home Assistant.
      </p>
      <p>
        Additional hardware integrations require development and testing. The reusable core and
        ESPHome integration provide a starting point; broader support for optional peripherals is
        still planned. The browser installer offers qualified releases for supported boards.
      </p>
      <p>For implementation details, see:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/OpenAthan-Project/openathan/blob/main/docs/development/architecture.md"
            >Firmware architecture and hardware adapters</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/OpenAthan-Project/openathan/blob/main/firmware/esphome/scheduler/README.md#controls-and-reuse"
            >Scheduler controls and reuse</a
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
  <section class="docs wrap" aria-labelledby="waveshare-setup">
    <div class="docs-body">
      <h2 id="waveshare-setup">Waveshare Box V2</h2>
      <p>
        {
          waveshareRelease
            ? `OpenAthan ${waveshareRelease.tag} is available for this model.`
            : 'Support is being prepared for v0.5.0 and becomes available after its qualified release is published.'
        }
        Use ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-1.85C-BOX V2; V1 is not supported.
      </p>
      <p>
        Connect its rear USB-C port using a USB data cable. Existing OpenAthan reports its hardware
        automatically. For a new installation, choose Waveshare Box V2 and confirm that existing
        firmware and saved data will be erased.
      </p>
      <p>
        Save Wi-Fi and a device password, then open device settings to choose your location, prayer
        settings and volume. The rear USB-C port also supplies normal power; RESET restarts the
        device. Touch and microphones are not supported.
      </p>
      <p>
        The onboard RTC restores previously synchronized time across powered restarts. First use
        needs internet time. Retention after unplugging, battery behavior and clock drift remain
        unqualified.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
